The interface language follows the language of the wiki you are on, not your account preferences: on the French Wikipedia the sidebar is in French.
On a wiki whose language has no translation yet, the sidebar stays in English, and the AI is asked to write its explanations in the language of the claim rather than in English. Because it is not told which language that is, it may still answer in English, especially when the cited source is in English.
Quotations from sources are always shown in the source's own language, exactly as they appear there.

On right-to-left wikis (Hebrew, Arabic, Persian, …) the sidebar opens on the left so it does not cover the navigation menus.

'''Would you like the tool in your language?''' The interface has about 200 short messages. Italian, Vietnamese and German are the most-used languages that do not have a translation yet.
Leave a note on the [[User talk:Alaexis/Source Verifier|talk page]] and we will send you the list, or open an issue on [https://github.com/alex-o-748/citation-checker-script/issues GitHub].

== Installation ==
The script is hosted on the English Wikipedia and can be loaded from any wiki.

=== On all wikis at once (recommended) ===
Add the following line to your [[Special:MyPage/global.js|global.js]] here on Meta.
It will then load on every Wikimedia wiki where you are logged in.
<syntaxhighlight lang="javascript">
mw.loader.load('https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Alaexis/AI_Source_Verification.js&action=raw&ctype=text/javascript');
</syntaxhighlight>

=== On a single wiki ===
Add the same line to <code>Special:MyPage/common.js</code> on the wiki where you want to use it.
On the English Wikipedia you can also use the "Install" button on the [[:en:User:Alaexis/AI Source Verification|English Wikipedia page]].

== Usage ==
* Open an article and click the '''Verify''' tab next to Article / Talk. On Vector legacy and Minerva, it is in the '''More''' menu.
* With the sidebar open, click any inline citation (for example <sup>[1]</sup>).
* Optionally choose an AI provider from the drop-down.
* Click '''Verify'''. The tool fetches the source and returns a verdict with an explanation.
* '''Verify all citations''' checks every reference in the article and produces a report that you can copy to the article's talk page.
* If a source is offline or paywalled, you can paste its text or upload a PDF in the sidebar.
* Under each result you can say whether the verdict was right. This helps us measure and improve the tool.

The tool works on articles, user pages and drafts.
In '''Settings''' you can choose whether the "claim" for a citation is only the sentence before it, or all the text back to the previous citation (default).

== AI providers ==
'''Free, no API key needed:''' open-weights models hosted on Wikimedia's own infrastructure ([https://wikitech.wikimedia.org/wiki/Machine_Learning/LiftWing/Large_Language_Models LiftWing]) and on the Wikimedia account at HuggingFace (the default), and by [https://publicai.co PublicAI].

'''Commercial, with your own API key:''' Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini.
These are noticeably more accurate.
Your key is stored only in your own browser (localStorage) and can be removed at any time.

== How accurate is it? ==
Only a small share of citations fail to support their claims: roughly 1 in 20 to 1 in 50, based on human reviews on the English Wikipedia.
The tool catches nearly all of them, but it also flags about 25–30% of good citations.
Because good citations are so much more common, '''most citations the tool flags will turn out to be fine'''.
Always read the source yourself before acting on a verdict.
[https://github.com/alex-o-748/citation-checker-script/blob/main/docs/full-provider-benchmark-2026-08-23.md Benchmark details].

These figures were measured on English Wikipedia articles.
We do not yet have a labelled benchmark for other languages, so accuracy elsewhere may be different.
If you have checked a batch of results on your wiki, we would very much like to hear what you found.

=== Limitations ===
* '''Online sources only.''' For paywalled, offline or book sources you can paste the text or upload a PDF.
* '''False positives.''' Many good citations are flagged, because of model errors or because the source could not be read properly (paywalls, video, JavaScript-heavy pages, very long documents).
* '''Accuracy varies by model.''' Commercial models do significantly better than the free ones.
* '''Limited PDF support''' for sources fetched from the web; scanned PDFs without a text layer cannot be read.

=== Out of scope ===
* '''Reliability''' of sources: the tool only checks whether a source supports the claim, not whether the source is any good.
* '''Copyright violations''': the tool does not check whether article text copies or closely paraphrases a source.

